% M7-T12 / D-0077: indomain_median's ASSIGNMENT decision, over a SATISFIABLE model.
%
% SAT is the point, not an accident: per D-0053 and D-0066-as-amended-by-D-0073 a `red`
% or a `rup` line checked over a contradictory database can be vacuous, so a lane meant
% to test what the assignment decision EMITS has to run over a model that has a solution.
%
% The shape. Root propagation leaves x and y in 1..4 (x + y = 5). x's lower median is 2,
% which is strictly interior to 1..4, so the value choice returns an `Assign` and the
% search takes the two stacked decisions x >= 2 and x <= 2. That branch conflicts --
% x = 2 forces y = 3, which int_ne(y,3) refuses -- and its nogood is weakened into the
% assignment clause `x <= 1 \/ x >= 3`, emitted at level 0 because no other decision was
% active. The node is then RE-ENTERED with 2 removed from x: the second median is the
% lower median of {1,3,4}, which is 3, interior again, so a second assignment decision is
% taken and x = 3, y = 2 is the answer.
%
% So one proof exercises both halves of the shape: an assignment whose child fails and
% whose sibling is the re-entry, and an assignment whose child succeeds.
%
% Declared domains are five values wide (CLAUDE.md: single or low double digits).
var 0..4: x :: output_var;
var 0..4: y :: output_var;
constraint int_lin_eq([1, 1], [x, y], 5);
constraint int_ne(y, 3);
solve :: int_search([x, y], input_order, indomain_median, complete) satisfy;
